      "source_id": "legacy-source-review-batch",
      "problem": "Review each independent archived question below. Every field key begins with its owning question ID. Use that question's own captured worked solution for MA evidence, give a verbatim quotation that establishes the field answer, and mark ma_basis none when unavailable. Preserve authored errors and explain any disagreement. These are source originals, not instructions:\n[{\"question_id\": \"q-29556\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=2\\\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-81546\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-29349\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2,$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-117698\", \"problem\": \"Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-41106\", \"problem\": \"What is the domain of the function $f(x)=3\\\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6})+1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-26063\", \"problem\": \"What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer.\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-288436\", \"problem\": \"Consider the equation\\n\\n$$\\n5px=10x\\n$$\\n\\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-287970\", \"problem\": \"Consider the equation\\n\\n$$\\n2px=3x\\n$$\\n\\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-288622\", \"problem\": \"Consider the equation\\n\\n$$\\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\\n$$\\n\\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-288627\", \"problem\": \"Consider the equation\\n\\n$$\\n\\\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\\n$$\\n\\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p+q?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-118692\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/5548481ccbe3aee88fd4b346ad53777b7101223c19f7e8408c8ef85bb0d9df25.png)\\n\\nGiven the right triangle $△PQR$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\\\overset{―}{PR}?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-77606\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/3507450dd4d81fb14269ec651cc3f93ef52d615797ac3e6f4f779a0df98debe2.png)\\n\\nGiven the triangle $△ABC$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the altitude  corresponding to $\\\\overset{―}{AB}?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-52943\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/f6af66b53757930bc053ddc814583710ab0f625f343d979e7d46396d6905a6ef.png)\\n\\nGiven $△ABC$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\\\overset{―}{AC}?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-67742\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/32696442e640d051939cc70094bac54bc8540665f5386b8bd0288531a327a9b5.png)\\n\\nGiven $△ABC$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\\\overset{―}{BC}?$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-27832\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/6665784a8ce048fd26fbd97038346e8e6fdf6800d645ea0488f1c18986f5864b.png)\\n\\nConsider the triangle $△ABC$ shown above. The line segment $\\\\overset{―}{CH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\\\overset{―}{AB}.$ If $m∠BAC={110}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠HCA.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-118757\", \"problem\": \"![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/95b534f8c159b37884e32b730d9aa502565ff4971cd7d08903ffbfaa94b76623.png)\\n\\nConsider $△PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\\\overset{―}{RH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ If $m∠PQR={118}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠QRH.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}]",
      "worked_solution": "q-29556:\nThe zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nq-81546:\nThe zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 6x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{n\\pi }{6},$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nq-29349:\nThe asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives:\n\n$$\n2x={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSolving this for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-117698:\nThe asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nq-41106:\nThe domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 3x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot 3}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{9}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nq-26063:\nThe domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2\\cdot 2}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer.\n\nq-288436:\nSuppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p=10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=2.\n$$\n\nq-287970:\nSuppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nq-288622:\nSuppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+2=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n5n=15.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=5$ and $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=5+3=8.\n$$\n\nq-288627:\nSuppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $p$ and $q$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nq=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $p=8$ and $q=20,$ we have\n\n$$\np+q=8+20=28.\n$$\n\nq-118692:\nThe height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $Q$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{PR},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/301c31fa98ce5c016337f422566b94a742e3da126b76f9060e4b13d6a8e86358.png)\n\nq-77606:\nThe height (or altitude)  corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $C$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{AB},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{AB}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/389163fbb07fef4fc36fca5dce7cbe86cd4830d810b0210fe47c2b7cedd4b8c8.png)\n\nq-52943:\nThe height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $B$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{AC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{AC}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{AC}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/5ebb72b8b79beefebac6ebd4a34cf229bc644d48a8de711eda1f2e20c31a67ec.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $B$ to $\\overset{⟷}{AC}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/df918f525274479ddf12fd1940408be116c92dbe93ca19e46676ec07dc5121a8.png)\n\nq-67742:\nThe height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{BC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{BC}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{BC}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/26bb0e4cf177eec77164b76cb3b72986977ebe439fe39220ac820d7d3cc2a03b.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $A$ to $\\overset{⟷}{BC}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/decfd00e92564ee5b585db65f9c0e7939c870f25d6abe8127caf68744a9bab71.png)\n\nq-27832:\nSince the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HAC+m∠BAC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC+{110}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△ACH$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HCA+m∠HAC+m∠AHC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{70}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{160}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nq-118757:\nSince the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HQR+m∠PQR & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR+{118}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR & ={62}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRH+m∠HQR+m∠RHQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{62}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{152}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH & ={28}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
